      "narrative": "The copper lamps hiss against the morning damp as Mara Venn looks up from her mug. She wipes a thumb along its rim, considering you with those sharp, flint-grey eyes.\n\n\"Old Bram,\" she says, and the name lands heavy. \"He worked the ferry across the Gorge for thirty years. Knew every current, every shoal, every whisper that crossed that water. Then, eight days ago, they found him facedown at the landing. His boat, the *Grey Hind*, still tied to the post. No wound. No struggle. Just... stopped.\"\n\nShe leans back, the chair groaning.\n\n\"The Kestrel Guild sealed the Gate the same morning. Said it was 'regulation.' Said there'd be an inquiry. But they haven't named a new ferryman, and they haven't let anyone through. Travellers have been stacking up at the Copper Finch for a week now, burning coin and patience.\"\n\nShe tilts her head, voice dropping lower.\n\n\"Toran there\"\u2014she nods toward the ink-stained clerk nursing something pale at the bar\u2014\"he kept the Gate's lock for years. Guild swore him to silence, but he's been jumpy as a startled hare since Bram died. Won't say why. Won't even look at the Gate.\"\n\nShe lets that hang, then fixes you with a direct look.\n\n\"So that's what I know. The question is\u2014what's your stake in it, stranger? Just passing through, or does the Ash Gate mean something to you?\""
    },
