      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the mutter of voices and the hiss of lamps. Morning light falls thin and grey through a sky the colour of old pewter. The street\u2014little more than packed earth and scattered stone\u2014slopes downhill toward the Gorge.\n\nA hundred yards ahead, the Ash Gate rises.\n\nIt's a cracked black arch, standing alone against the pale sky. No wall flanks it, no fence extends from either side\u2014just the arch, as if someone drove a splinter of night into the earth and left it there. The stone is veined with pale scars, wind-worn and ancient. Beyond it, the Gorge road drops into mist.\n\nThe landing platform extends from the Gate's base: broad flagstones, dark with damp, leading to a wooden dock that juts over nothing\u2014the Gorge below is hidden in fog, but you hear it: a low, constant churn of water far down.\n\nA figure stands at the edge of the platform.\n\nTravel-stained cloak. Satchel slung across one shoulder. He's not checking a strap this time\u2014he's just standing there, staring through the arch at the mist beyond, as if waiting for something to emerge from it.\n\nHe hasn't noticed you yet."
    },
